{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774496036,"nonce":"rcqPU9VcG3WfCl+xk90bCVmyTtM=","title":"Review SimpleFeedbackState: post-hoc view layer needs documentation and possible redesign","message":"SimpleFeedbackState (bodies.py:38) is a Module that provides named-field access to state history for loss computation and analysis. It is NOT the runtime state representation — during lax.scan execution, state is stored in equinox.nn.State (a flat dict keyed by StateIndex markers).\n\nSimpleFeedbackState is constructed post-hoc by state_view_fn (bodies.py:232) at each timestep during the scan, assembling per-node states into a readable view. This view is what gets stacked into the state history and passed to the loss function.\n\nThis architecture is confusing because:\n1. SimpleFeedbackState looks like a traditional nested state class, but it's only a view\n2. It's unclear from the code alone that the runtime uses State (flat dict) while the history uses SimpleFeedbackState (nested Module)\n3. The relationship between state_view_fn, Graph.state_view(), and the scan carry is not documented\n\nThe user wants to review this design and consider:\n- Whether SimpleFeedbackState should be documented more clearly as a view\n- Whether the view construction could be optimized (it's called at every scan timestep)\n- Whether this pattern should be made more explicit in the architecture docs\n- How this interacts with selective trajectory storage / streaming loss (if we stream the loss inside the scan, we may not need the view at all during training)\n\nCross-ref: Bug 96a0725 (static JAX arrays), memory optimization discussion in rlrmp session 31fa1977.","files":null}]}